# Both functions have a guard that a parsed record fails, and a slow version that holds a loop. The loop of counts() has a counter, a relation and a sum: there the walk has something to reckon, so that slow version is walked. The loop of
# follows() only reads names and asks for truth: no operator on numbers, no relation: a walk would reckon nothing there and only cost bytes and time of the build: that slow version is written straight, as one without a loop is.
# f1 = counts, f2 = follows
